CLAIMS=[
('The average new pickup costs $62,000; King Ranch/Longhorn trims $75,000-95,000.','The average new pickup truck in America now costs $62,000',SN+'KBBDEC: Cox/KBB December 2025 full-size pickup ATP ~$66,386 (secondary coverage of the Cox report; the primary page was not opened). $62,000 is below the full-size figure; all-pickup averages including midsize may be lower. Trim range not checked. Approximately right, unverified.',['KBBDEC']),
('The median household income in America is $84,000.','The median household income in America is $84,000',P+'CENSUS2024: Census P60-286 (released 9 September 2025): "Median household income was $83,730 in 2024." Verified (rounded).',['CENSUS2024']),
('Payment $1,247/month over 84 months.','It\'s $1,247',C+'At 7.2% over 84 months, $1,247 corresponds to ~$82,090 financed; a $90,000 loan would be $1,367 and $87,000 would be $1,322. Internally inconsistent with the $87,000-90,000 truck.',[]),
('Fuel: 15 mpg vs 35 mpg, 15,000 miles, $3.50/gal: $3,500 vs $1,500, a $2,000/year ($167/month) difference.','Your F25 gets roughly 15 m per gallon',C+'15,000 / 15 x 3.50 = $3,500; 15,000 / 35 x 3.50 = $1,500; $2,000 / 12 = $166.67. Reproduces. '+R+'Heavy-duty F-250s have no EPA mpg rating; 15 mpg is an assumption.',[]),
('Monthly total: 1,247 + 167 + 60 + 75 + 100 + 80 = $1,729.','Your total monthly cost of truck ownership is now $1,729',C+'Sum reproduces ($1,729). The insurance, tyre, maintenance and registration increments are assumptions, not sourced.',[]),
('Financed at 7.2% over 84 months on $90,000, you pay $14,748 in interest.','You\'re paying $14,748',C+'Wrong. $90,000 at 7.2% over 84 months gives $24,841 of interest (payment $1,367); even the implied $82,090 principal at $1,247 gives $22,658. $14,748 understates interest by about 40%.',[]),
('Three-year-old Camry for $28,000: payment ~$450, insurance $180, gas $125, total ~$850; difference $879/month, $10,548/year, $73,836 over 7 years ("$83,000").','The difference between driving the truck and driving the sedan is $879',C+'1,729 - 850 = 879; x 12 = 10,548; x 84 = 73,836 (reproduce). The spoken "$83,000" contradicts $73,836. A $450 payment on $28,000 at 7.2% implies ~78 months (60 months = $557). The $125 Camry gas figure matches the script\'s own 35 mpg calculation ($1,500 / 12).',[]),
('$879/month invested at 8% for 7 years gives about $95,000.','after 7 years, you\'d have $95,000',C+'$879/month at 8% compounded monthly for 84 months = $98,548. "$95,000" is slightly understated; approximately reproduces.',[]),
('Lenders want DTI under 43%; the truck payment alone is 26% of an $85,000 salary; later: 18% of gross income at $7,000/month.','That truck payment alone eats up 26%',C+'$1,247 x 12 / $85,000 = 17.6%, not 26% (even the full $1,729 is 24.4%). The script\'s later "18%" of $7,000/month (17.8%) is right and contradicts the 26%. '+R+'The 43% DTI cap was the original CFPB Qualified Mortgage limit, replaced by a price-based test in 2021; many lenders still use ~43-50%.',[]),
('Six-month emergency fund ~$18,000 at $200/month takes 7.5 years; a $6,000 card balance at 23% costs $115/month interest.','7 and 1/2 years to build that emergency fund',C+'18,000 / 200 = 90 months = 7.5 years; 6,000 x 23% / 12 = $115. Reproduce.',[]),
('Trucks lose ~40% in 5 years vs 55% for sedans: $36,000 vs $15,400; after 7 years the truck is worth ~$45,000 and the sedan ~$12,000.','A truck might lose 40% of its value in 5 years',C+'0.40 x 90,000 = 36,000; 0.55 x 28,000 = 15,400 (reproduce; ratio 2.34x, "double" understates slightly). '+R+'Lower pickup depreciation than sedans matches iSeeCars-type studies, but sedans like the Camry typically depreciate less than 55% in 5 years; not fetched.',[]),
('Renting a U-Haul at $29.99 six times a year saves $80,000 over seven years; the truck costs $80,000-120,000 more than a sensible alternative; later "$40,000 extra".','You could rent a U-Haul for $29.99',C+'Rental cost ~$1,260 over 7 years; the saving figure is the truck premium, which the script itself computed as $73,836 (or $98,548 with returns). The $80,000-120,000 range and the later "$40,000 extra" are inconsistent with each other and with the script\'s own math. Switching from $1,100 to $350 ("$750") also contradicts the earlier $1,247 and $450.',[]),
('Hedonic adaptation; Veblen goods are products whose demand rises with price.','There\'s a term in economics called Vevelin goods',R+'Both concepts are real (Veblen 1899; Brickman et al.). Applying Veblen-good status to pickups is a framing, not evidence. Not refetched.',[]),
('A vehicle loses $15,000 the moment it leaves the lot.','loses $15,000 in value the moment',R+'Immediate depreciation of roughly 10% is a common rule of thumb; no source given. '+U,[]),
]
TITLE='Authentic title "The \'Pavement Princess\' Trap (And Why You\'re Broke)" uses slang in quotes, a "trap" frame and a direct second-person accusation. The script delivers a cost teardown of a luxury pickup used for commuting vs a used Camry. STRONG against the cohort. Association only.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). White background. Left: the recurring cartoon man (balding, goatee, white shirt) shrugging with a smug smile. Right: a heavy black two-line headline "EGO PURCHASE" and a curved arrow pointing at a beige crew-cab heavy-duty pickup with a red glow outline. At 320x180 the headline, shrug and truck stay legible. The thumbnail names the motive ("ego") rather than the title\'s slang, which works as a complementary pairing.'
SCRIPT='A cost-teardown rant: hook ($87,000 F-250 King Ranch at a red light); channel pitch; purchase prices and median income; dealer payment over 84 months; fuel, insurance, tyres, maintenance, registration; total monthly cost; interest; used-Camry comparison; 7-year gap and invested alternative; DTI and housing; skipped vacation; emergency fund; credit-card spiral; relationship strain; career risk aversion; hedonic adaptation; parallel universes; "I use my truck" objection and U-Haul; marketing and dealers; boring-sedan prescription; depreciation myth; "I might need it"; Veblen goods; sell-it advice; close. Verified: Census 2024 median household income $83,730. Reproduces: fuel math, monthly sum, 7-year difference, emergency fund, card interest, depreciation. Wrong: interest ($14,748 vs ~$24,841), DTI (26% vs 17.6%), and several internally inconsistent totals ($73,836 vs "$83,000", "$80,000-120,000", "$40,000"; $1,247/$450 vs $1,100/$350). Most cost increments are assumptions. The stored ending is complete.'
